- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand: Stop using force to crack down on APEC protests","publisher":"Amnesty International Thailand","publication_date":"2022-11-19","url":"https://www.amnesty.or.th/en/news/2022/11/thailand-stop-using-force-to-crack-down-on-apec-protests/","claim":"Amnesty said the 18 November APEC crackdown caused at least nine injuries among protesters and four reporters and led to 25 arrests, describing the response as violent suppression of peaceful assembly.","locator":"opening paragraph and lines 54-67","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct human-rights monitoring organization with specific event counts and a clear rights-based interpretation, useful as independent corroboration of Reuters.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut government through the crowd-control police; direct personal orders are not shown, so responsibility is institutional and supervisory rather than purely personal.","contrary_evidence":["Amnesty reports both injuries to protesters and injuries to officers, and the event involved a protest against the summit."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
- I reviewed roughly 30 candidate documents across Reuters/Euronews, Reuters Institute, Citizen Lab, Amnesty, ISEAS, Thai Journalists Association, Nation, Reuters-syndicated local outlets, and several search-result-only hits.
- Rejected as duplicates or low-yield: ReutersConnect image-only pages, syndication mirrors that added no new factual claim, and 2026 local-election pages that were out of period.
- Rejected as already covered by the local structured prior: Freedom House, V-Dem, RSF, and other structured dataset pages that would have repeated supplied priors rather than add ruler-specific narrative.
- Rejected as too weak or too indirect for final use: older 2020-2021 protest explainers unless they were needed only for retrospective context, and pages that returned cache-miss/internal-error with no usable text.
- Kept only the sources that added non-duplicative ruler-period evidence: election contestability, rule-change disputes, term-limit litigation, media self-censorship, surveillance, security reshuffles, and protest policing.

Remaining gaps
- 4B.4 is still thin on direct proof that Prayut strengthened independent courts, legislatures, audit bodies, or local government; the record mainly shows those bodies constraining him or his coalition politicizing appointments.
- 4B.7 remains weak on political equality for minorities, women, and excluded groups in 2022; I did not find a strong, ruler-specific source that tied those outcomes to Prayut's direct choices.
- 4B.2 would be stronger with direct Thai-language parliamentary or constitutional text on the election-system amendment sponsors, rather than relying on Reuters reporting of an opposition challenge.
- 4B.9 would benefit from a direct official or forensic source naming the operational chain behind surveillance and any 2022 digital-control orders beyond monarchy-sensitive speech warnings and the Pegasus report.

